[Printed, p. 1514 (PDF p. 13)] “By Lemma A.5 in Lei and Ding (2021), the variance can be upper-bounded as $$\mathrm{Var}\big[(*)\big] \le \Big(\frac{1}{p_Cn_Tn}\Big)^2\frac{n_Tn_C}{n(n-1)}\sum_{i,j}\Big(x_i'\Big(x_j\big(e_j(1) - e_j(0)\big) - \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\big(e_i(1) - e_i(0)\big)\Big)\Big)^2$$ $$\le \Big(\frac{1}{p_Cn_Tn}\Big)^2\frac{n_Tn_C}{n(n-1)}\sum_{i,j}\big(x_i'x_j\big(e_j(1) - e_j(0)\big)\big)^2$$ $$= \Big(\frac{1}{p_Cn_Tn}\Big)^2n^2\frac{n_Tn_C}{n(n-1)}\sum_in^{-1}\|x_i\|_2^2\big(e_j(1) - e_j(0)\big)^2 = O\Big(\frac{\kappa}{n}\Big),$$ where, for the second inequality, we use the fact that, for each $x_i$, $\frac1n\sum_j(x_i'x_j(e_j(1) - e_j(0)))^2 \ge \frac1n\sum_j(x_i'(x_j(e_j(1) - e_j(0)) - \frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nx_j(e_j(1) - e_j(0))))^2$. For the second to last equality, we use the fact $\sum_{i,j}(x_i'x_ja_j)^2 = \sum_ja_j^2x_j'(\sum_ix_ix_i')x_j = n\sum_ja_j^2\|x_j\|_2^2$ by Assumption 2. Thus, the term $(*)$ is of order $O_p(\sqrt{\frac{\kappa d}{n}})$.”
[Sol's corrected text (validated.json, P005-E007, prior_correction.corrected_text), verbatim] “$\sum_j n^{-1}\|x_j\|_2^2(e_j(1)-e_j(0))^2$.”
[Corrected third line] $$= \Big(\frac{1}{p_Cn_Tn}\Big)^2n^2\frac{n_Tn_C}{n(n-1)}\sum_jn^{-1}\|x_j\|_2^2\big(e_j(1) - e_j(0)\big)^2 = O\Big(\frac{\kappa}{n}\Big).$$
[Mathematical content, with every hypothesis] Fix $n \ge 2$ and $1 \le n_T \le n-1$; let $x_1, \ldots, x_n \in \mathbb{R}^d$ with $\sum_ix_ix_i' = nI_d$ (Assumption 2 (2)); let $\delta_j = e_j(1) - e_j(0)$ (any real numbers $\delta_1, \ldots, \delta_n$ will do in (a)–(d)), and $\mu = \frac1n\sum_kx_k\delta_k$. Then
(a) for each $i$: $\sum_j\big(x_i'(x_j\delta_j - \mu)\big)^2 \le \sum_j(x_i'x_j\delta_j)^2$ (the second inequality of the display);
(b) $\sum_{i,j}(x_i'x_ja_j)^2 = \sum_ja_j^2x_j'\big(\sum_ix_ix_i'\big)x_j = n\sum_ja_j^2\|x_j\|_2^2$ for all real $a_1, \ldots, a_n$;
(c) hence $\Big(\frac{1}{p_Cn_Tn}\Big)^2\frac{n_Tn_C}{n(n-1)}\sum_{i,j}(x_i'x_j\delta_j)^2 = \Big(\frac{1}{p_Cn_Tn}\Big)^2n^2\frac{n_Tn_C}{n(n-1)}\sum_jn^{-1}\|x_j\|_2^2\delta_j^2 = \frac{1}{p_Tp_Cn(n-1)}\cdot\frac1n\sum_jh_j\delta_j^2$;
(d) and, since $h_j \le n\kappa$, this is at most $\frac{\kappa}{p_Tp_C(n-1)}\cdot\frac1n\sum_j\delta_j^2$;
(e) [order] along a sequence of populations satisfying Assumptions 1–3, $\frac1n\sum_j\delta_j^2 = O(1)$ (because $\frac1n\sum_ie_i^2(1) = O(1)$ and $\frac1n\sum_ie_i^2(0) = O(1)$, p. 1513), so the quantity in (c) is $O(\frac{\kappa}{n})$, as printed.
As printed, the third line sums over $i$ while the residual difference carries the free index $j$; the leverage and the residual must carry the same index.

[Printed, p. 1510 (PDF p. 9)] “THEOREM 4.2: Under Assumption 2, an unbiased estimator for the bias of the interacted ATE estimator is $$\widehat{\mathrm{Bias}}_I = \frac{n_T}{(n-2)n_C}(\overline{hy(0)}_C - \bar h_C\overline{y(0)}_C) + \bar x_C'\big(\widehat L_C^{-1} - I_d^{-1}\big)\hat N_C - \frac{n_C}{(n-2)n_T}(\overline{hy(1)}_T - \bar h_T\overline{y(1)}_T) - \bar x_T'\big(\widehat L_T^{-1} - I_d^{-1}\big)\hat N_T.$$ The estimator $\widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_{I,\mathrm{Debiased}} = \widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_I - \widehat{\mathrm{Bias}}_I$ is unbiased for estimating the ATE.” (The theorem continues with the asymptotic expansion, which is P005-T27.)
[The statement with its hypotheses made explicit] Let $n \ge 3$ and $1 \le n_T \le n-1$, under complete randomization; let $\bar x = 0$ and $\overline{xx'} = I_d$ (Assumption 2 at this $n$); let $\widehat L_T$ and $\widehat L_C$ be invertible under every assignment (footnote 4); the potential outcomes are arbitrary fixed numbers. With $\widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_I = \overline{y(1)}_T - \overline{y(0)}_C - (\bar x_T'\widehat\beta_{I,T} - \bar x_C'\widehat\beta_{I,C})$, $\widehat\beta_{I,T} = \widehat L_T^{-1}\widehat N_T$, $\widehat\beta_{I,C} = \widehat L_C^{-1}\widehat N_C$ as in Section 4 (by Lin (2013), as the paper cites it, $\widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_I$ is the OLS coefficient $\widehat\tau_I$ of the interacted regression (2)), and $\widehat{\mathrm{Bias}}_I$ as displayed:
(i) $\mathbf{E}[\widehat{\mathrm{Bias}}_I] = \mathbf{E}[\widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_I] - \mathrm{ATE}$;
(ii) $\mathbf{E}[\widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_{I,\mathrm{Debiased}}] = \mathrm{ATE} = \overline{y(1)} - \overline{y(0)}$, where $\widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_{I,\mathrm{Debiased}} = \widehat{\mathrm{ATE}}_I - \widehat{\mathrm{Bias}}_I = \Big(\overline{y(1)}_T - \bar x_T'\widehat N_T + \frac{n_C}{(n-2)n_T}\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}_T(h, y(1))\Big) - \Big(\overline{y(0)}_C - \bar x_C'\widehat N_C + \frac{n_T}{(n-2)n_C}\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}_C(h, y(0))\Big)$;
(iii) arm by arm: $\mathbf{E}\big[\overline{y(1)}_T - \bar x_T'\widehat N_T + \frac{n_C}{(n-2)n_T}\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}_T(h, y(1))\big] = \overline{y(1)}$ and $\mathbf{E}\big[\overline{y(0)}_C - \bar x_C'\widehat N_C + \frac{n_T}{(n-2)n_C}\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}_C(h, y(0))\big] = \overline{y(0)}$ (these two need only $\bar x = 0$, $n \ge 3$ and $n_T \ge 2$, respectively $n_C \ge 2$).
